On Fri, Dec 19, 2014 at 2:19 PM, Chris Paciorek <paci...@stat.berkeley.edu> wrote:
> Hi Shivaram, thanks.
>
> Yes, creating a bash script for /usr/bin/realpath worked for me. If
> you guys could either post instructions for that on the SparkR webpage
> (where you currently make a note about running R CMD javareconf) or
> even better modify spark-ec2, that would be very helpful.
>
> More generally, it would also be helpful if you all posted the full
> set of commands a user on EC2 would need to install SparkR on the
> SparkR webpage, including how to install rJava, and how to make sure
> things are set up on both master and workers.
>
> chris
>
> On Wed, Dec 17, 2014 at 4:53 PM, Shivaram Venkataraman
> <shivaram.v...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > I've run into this before and the problem is the error caused by the
> > following lines
> >
> > /usr/lib64/R/bin/javareconf: line 250: /usr/bin/realpath: No such file or
> > directory
> > /bin/sed: -e expression #1, char 0: no previous regular expression
> >
> > I found a workaround at
> > http://superuser.com/questions/771104/usr-bin-realpath-not-found-in-centos-6-5
> > which suggests using readlink -e instead of realpath.
> > I think I just edited javareconf to fix things, but you could also create an
> > alias or make a simple bash script named realpath which just calls readlink
> > -e
> >
> > Let me know if this works for you and I can try and add this fix to the
> > Spark EC2 setup scripts
> >
> > Thanks
> > Shivaram
> >
> > On Wed, Dec 17, 2014 at 4:44 PM, <paci...@berkeley.edu> wrote:
> >>
> >> So I was using SparkR a few weeks ago on EC2 and things were fine. I was
> >> starting up a cluster via the spark-ec2 script in Spark 1.1.0 and then I was
> >> installing SparkR as follows after logging onto the master node:
> >>
> >> cd /root
> >> wget http://cran.cnr.berkeley.edu/src/contrib/rJava_0.9-6.tar.gz
> >> /root/spark-ec2/copy-dir rJava_0.9-6.tar.gz
> >> R CMD javareconf
> >> tar xvzf rJava_0.9-6.tar.gz; R CMD INSTALL rJava
